'''Ferro Balboa, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Ferro Balboa}}; "Balboa Iron"), commonly abbreviated '''FBL''', is a Balboan {{w|steel}} company and the second-largest steel producer in [[Balboa]]. The company operates a pure {{w|electric arc furnace}} (EAF) steelworks at [[Sant Bart]], in the interior highlands of Balboa, using {{w|scrap metal}} and the country's abundant low-cost {{w|hydroelectricity|hydropower}}. FBL was established in 1982 when the state-owned [[Acereria Nacional de Balboa]] (ANB) was privatised and split into two companies: FBL inherited the Sant Bart EAF plant, while [[Acereries de l'Istme]] (ACI) inherited the integrated steelworks at [[Les Drassanes]].

FBL is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (SCX: FBL) and is a constituent of the [[SCX 100]] index. The company produces approximately 4.5 million tonnes of {{w|crude steel}} per year, accounting for 31 per cent of total Balboan steel production.

== History ==

=== Origins in the Acereria Nacional de Balboa ===

FBL's origins lie in the [[Acereria Nacional de Balboa]] (ANB), the state-owned steel conglomerate established in 1949 with [[Adonian Community]] reconstruction loans. In 1953, the ANB established a second steelworks at [[Sant Bart]], the rump state capital in the interior highlands, using a small {{w|electric arc furnace}} (EAF) powered by hydropower from the nearby [[Yalabirga]] river system. The Sant Bart plant was modest — producing approximately 100,000 tonnes per year — but it demonstrated the viability of EAF steelmaking in Balboa, where cheap hydropower could offset the cost of imported {{w|scrap metal}}.

After the reunification of Balboa in 1973, the ANB invested in modernisation, upgrading the Sant Bart EAF capacity. By 1980, the Sant Bart plant produced approximately 500,000 tonnes per year.

=== Privatisation (1982) ===

In 1982, as part of the broader privatisation programme, the Balboan government privatised the ANB. The company was restructured and split into two independent companies:

* '''[[Acereries de l'Istme]]''' (ACI), which inherited the integrated steelworks at [[Les Drassanes]].
* '''Ferro Balboa''' (FBL), which inherited the Sant Bart EAF plant and was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1984.

FBL was established as a pure EAF producer, taking advantage of Balboa's cheap hydropower and growing domestic scrap metal supply. Unlike ACI, FBL does not operate {{w|blast furnace}}s and therefore does not require imported {{w|iron ore}} or {{w|coking coal}} — a significant cost advantage that insulates the company from raw material price volatility.

=== Expansion (1984–present) ===

Through the 1990s and 2000s, FBL expanded its EAF capacity at Sant Bart, adding two more furnaces and a new {{w|rolling (metalworking)|bar rolling mill}}. The company grew from approximately 500,000 tonnes per year at privatisation to 4.5 million tonnes per year by 2026, driven by domestic construction demand and the competitive advantage of EAF steelmaking powered by Balboa's hydropower.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 temporarily reduced demand from the construction sector but FBL maintained production, supported by the domestic construction boom that followed the Balboan government's infrastructure stimulus programme.

By 2026, FBL produced approximately 4.5 million tonnes of crude steel per year and employed approximately 5,000 people.

== Operations ==

FBL operates a pure EAF steelworks at [[Sant Bart]], in the interior highlands of Balboa. The facility comprises:

* '''4 × {{w|electric arc furnace}}s (EAF)''': Produce {{w|crude steel}} from {{w|scrap metal}}.
* '''{{w|rolling (metalworking)|Bar rolling mill}}''': Produces long products for the construction and infrastructure sectors.

The Sant Bart location provides access to the interior domestic market and the [[Yalabirga]] hydropower system, which supplies the plant's electricity. The EAF route is highly electricity-intensive, and Balboa's low hydropower costs — among the cheapest in Adonia — make FBL's steelmaking significantly cheaper than competitors in countries that rely on fossil fuel power generation. This advantage is amplified by the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] on manufactured exports, which effectively subsidises the cost of electricity for export-oriented producers.

== Products ==

FBL's product mix is focused on long products for the construction and infrastructure sectors:

* '''{{w|Rebar|Reinforcing bar}} (rebar)''': The company's primary product, used in construction and infrastructure projects.
* '''{{w|Steel bar|Merchant bar}}''': Used in fabrication and structural applications.
* '''{{w|I-beam|Structural sections}} (I-beams, H-beams, angles)''': Used in construction and infrastructure.
* '''{{w|Wire rod}}''': Used in construction, fasteners, and wire products.

FBL also produces a small volume of specialty steel grades for the agricultural equipment and automotive supply chains.

== Raw materials ==

FBL operates entirely on the EAF route and does not require {{w|iron ore}} or {{w|coking coal}}:

* '''{{w|Scrap metal}}''': Sourced from domestic collection (approximately 60 per cent) and imports from [[Potocsí]] and [[Tapuya]] within the [[BTPMEU]] (approximately 40 per cent). The Balboan government has invested in scrap metal collection infrastructure to support the EAF steel industry.
* '''Electricity''': Supplied by the [[Yalabirga]] hydropower system.
* '''{{w|Graphite electrode}}s''': Supplied by [[Shoko Denko Balboa]] (SD Balboa), which produces synthetic graphite from [[petroleum coke]] — a byproduct of [[CIEA]]'s [[Badia Curta]] refinery.

== Ownership ==

FBL is publicly traded on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]]. The ownership structure reflects the company's origin as a state-owned enterprise privatised in 1982:

* '''[[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB)''': 8% — institutional investor
* '''[[Balboa Pension Fund]] (BPF)''': 5% — institutional anchor investor
* '''Public float''': approximately 87% — held by institutional and retail investors

